refully.\n\nCalculating the normalized values reveals subtle nuances in the distribution. The first distribution shows New Haven at 0.4819 and Hartford at 0.5181, while the second distribution reverses this pattern. I'll compute the logarithmic comparisons systematically, tracking each component of the Kullback-Leibler divergence calculation.\n\nThe key steps involve computing logarithmic ratios between the distributions, carefully tracking how the values change when reversed. I'll methodically work through each calculation to confirm the precise mathematical relationship between these normalized percentage increases.\n","signature":""},{"type":"toolRequest","id":"call_94a2034de59b4c5cbdb685f4","toolCall":{"status":"success","value":{"name":"shell","arguments":{"command":"python3 -c \"\nimport math\n# From the table: percentage increase from 1942 to 1943\nnew_haven_1943 = 17.3\nhartford_1943 = 18.6\n\n# First distribution: normalize the percentage increases (New Haven, Hartford)\ntotal = new_haven_1943 + hartford_1943\nP = [new_haven_1943/total, hartford_1943/total]\n\n# Second distribution: reverse the order (Hartford, New Haven)\nQ = [hartford_1943/total, new_haven_1943/total]\n\n# KL divergence KL(P || Q)\nkl = sum(P[i] * math.log(P[i] / Q[i]) for i in range(len(P)))\nprint(f'P = {P}')\nprint(f'Q = {Q}')\nprint(f'KL(P || Q) = {kl}')\nprint(f'Answer to 5 decimal places: {kl:.5f}')\n\""}}},"_meta":{"goose_extension":"developer"}}],"metadata":{"userVisible":true,"agentVisible":true}}}
